WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.370
SPEAKER 1: Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE,

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.320
is Google's secured
and fully managed

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.090
Kubernetes service
that now includes

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.310
a revolutionary autopilot mode.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.430
It allows you to build, deploy,
and run fast, reliable apps

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:12.332
and services.

00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:14.790
But how do you ensure that the
infrastructure you provision

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.640
to run those services remains
reliable and available

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.750
for developers?

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.840
Today, I'm going to show you
how to manage GKE infrastructure

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.150
at scale with Cloud Operations.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.880
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.860
Imagine getting a
ticket like this one.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.690
You know that a service
is having an issue,

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.130
and the service
owners are asking

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.900
you to help them
determine whether it's

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.940
being caused by the GKE
infrastructure they're

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.900
running on.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.940
The problem is that
there's a default namespace

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.680
in every cluster in your org.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.020
How can you possibly find
the one you're after?

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.530
Let's see how Cloud
Operations can help.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.190
From the monitoring
overview screen,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.850
let's click on
the GKE dashboard.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.620
By looking at the
last six hours of data

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.900
and expanding the
alert timeline,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.340
we can in fact
confirm that there

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.770
are no alerts in the
default namespace.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.420
We can also confirm
that there aren't

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.992
any issues with our nodes.

00:01:06.992 --> 00:01:09.200
Let's have a look at the
workloads in that namespace,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.180
to see if there are
any issues there.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.540
We can find the workload
by clicking on View All

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.040
and then searching
for the namespace

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.165
where we know it's running.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.010
It looks like there is
a memory utilization

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.447
alert for this workload.

00:01:25.447 --> 00:01:28.030
Let's check to see if there are
any infrastructure issues that

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.060
may be causing this.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.000
Let's click on metrics
and select Memory

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.390
under Node Metrics.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.480
Here, we can see that the nodes
aren't having any memory alerts

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.445
or issues.

00:01:40.445 --> 00:01:41.820
So we can rule
out infrastructure

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.310
as a probable cause for
this particular incident,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.080
and we can reply to
the original message,

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.750
letting them know that
memory issues are constrained

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.258
to their specific service.

00:01:51.258 --> 00:01:53.550
Being able to take a very
limited amount of information

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.092
about a problem and
quickly determine

00:01:55.092 --> 00:01:57.760
whether an infrastructure
issue is at fault is fantastic.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.288
As an operator, your time
is incredibly valuable,

00:02:00.288 --> 00:02:02.580
and being able to quickly
eliminate your infrastructure

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.290
as a culprit is great.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.970
But what about
the service owner?

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.662
Let's have a look
at another example.

00:02:08.662 --> 00:02:10.120
Does your inbox
look like this when

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.830
there's a problem of any kind?

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.140
This might happen if there's
an alert flood caused

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.758
by a widespread issue, or it
might happen just because there

00:02:16.758 --> 00:02:18.550
are a lot of alerts
that might be triggered

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.290
for any number of reasons.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.122
How can you quickly
dig through this mess

00:02:22.122 --> 00:02:23.830
and figure out what's
actually important,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.120
and whether your
service is impacted?

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.580
Let's see how Cloud
Operations can help.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.830
Let's see if we can figure
out whether our service was

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.020
impacted by an
infrastructure issue.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.530
From the monitoring
overview screen,

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.440
let's go to the GKE
dashboard and have a look

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.640
at the behavior of our
system over the last day.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.290
When we expand the
alerts timeline,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.660
we can see that there
are two node alerts.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.580
Let's have a look at
the resource associated

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.820
with the alert.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.460
We can see that we are
getting high CPU alerts.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.040
In the metrics tab, which
can confirm elevated CPU

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.020
utilization of the node.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.470
When we look at
container metrics,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.387
we can see that there
are a number of services

00:03:00.387 --> 00:03:02.480
that are running on
this particular node.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.550
Some of them did not see
increased utilization

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.360
during interval.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.340
However, at least
two services did

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:10.190
see elevated CPUs that
corresponds to elevated CPU

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.020
utilization on the node.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.290
So we can confirm that not
only was our service impacted

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.540
by increased resource
utilization of the node,

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.960
but another service was as well.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.370
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.430
We saw how Cloud Operations
can help you quickly

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.380
isolate or eliminate
infrastructure issues

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.430
from a limited set of data.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.040
We also saw how to
use the timeline

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.010
to quickly filter alerts to
determine whether your service

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.612
is having an issue or not.

00:03:34.612 --> 00:03:36.820
I hope this helps you to
keep your GKE infrastructure

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.740
reliable and available.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:39.920
Thanks so much for watching.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.087
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:44.680
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.560
See you soon.

